**Physics checkpoint — entropy and process direction, 1 November 2026.**

Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult this entrance’s current scope and next steps before acting.

The sixth physics block is installed after `@physics-electric-review`. Nine new lessons and five reused thermal Records form the continuation: `physics-process-direction`, `th-reversibility`, `physics-entropy-state`, `th-entropy-balance`, `physics-reference-paths`, `th-entropy-properties`, `physics-cycle-bound`, `th-cycles`, `th-carnot`, `physics-irreversible-paths`, `physics-finite-bodies`, `physics-entropy-evidence`, `physics-entropy-study`, `physics-entropy-review`. Physics now has **77 common learning Records**. The five shared Records retain their original identities, prose, imports, source assignments and specialist continuations; appended notes define the bounded common reading. Existing mathematical differentials and logarithm/exponential preparation, energy/path lessons and inquiry evidence remain contextual reuse. General chemistry's entropy Record is available for the next chemistry block and was not given a competing edge here.

The block states a macroscopic second-law premise explicitly. It derives entropy path independence from the reversible cycle integral and generation from an actual path plus reversible return. It distinguishes closed-system heat transfer at boundary temperatures from system state changes, and distinguishes reservoir idealizations from finite bodies. Under fixed composition, simple compressibility and reversible pressure-volume work, T dS=dU+p dV connects to constant-capacity and ideal-gas state models. Actual irreversible heat and work are not identified with their reversible reference differentials. Boundary temperatures may coincide with system or reservoir temperatures under appropriate premises; no universal equality or inequality is asserted.

For an ideal gas with A=nR and total constant C_V, ΔS=C_V ln(T₂/T₁)+A ln(V₂/V₁), equivalently (C_V+A)ln(T₂/T₁)−A ln(p₂/p₁). Two reference routes have this same entropy change and different heat/work. The original A=2 J/K,C_V=3 J/K,200→400 K,1→2 m³ example has ΔU=600 J, ΔS=5 ln2 J/K and works 800 ln2 or 400 ln2 J. At fixed T with volume ratio r≥1, reversible work AT ln r, constant-pressure work AT(1−1/r) and insulated free-expansion work zero share the same endpoint ΔS=A ln r. The constant-pressure comparison explicitly requires its stated work and reservoir heat to be the only net exchanges, with auxiliary parts having no net energy/entropy storage. Its generation A(ln r−1+1/r) is nonnegative by a derivative proof; real-gas and other boundary models need a new account.

The two-reservoir cyclic bound W≤Q_h(1−T_c/T_h) follows from both balances, with no power claim. At T_h=400 K,T_c=100 K,Q_h=200 J, work 150 J is the reversible bound; a 175 J proposal closes energy but gives −1/4 J/K composite entropy change. An ideal-gas Carnot example with A=C_V=1 J/K follows (T,V)=(400,1),(400,2),(100,8),(100,4), with constant TV on its adiabats and net work 300 ln2 J. These are idealized comparisons, not apparatus demonstrations.

For two equal constant-capacity bodies initially at T_h>T_c>0, an insulated composite with cyclic work device and a common final temperature gives direct equilibrium T_d=(T_h+T_c)/2 and entropy change C ln[T_d²/(T_h T_c)]. The entropy constraint gives T_f≥sqrt(T_h T_c); energy then bounds work by C[T_h+T_c−2sqrt(T_h T_c)]. An infinitesimal reversible comparison keeps T_h T_c constant and reaches equality. At 400 K and 100 K,C=1 J/K, direct contact gives 250 K with no work and generation 2 ln(5/4) J/K; reversible extraction gives 200 K and 100 J. The exact family T_h=a²T₀,T_c=b²T₀ gives T_r=abT₀ and W_max=C T₀(a−b)². These bounds depend on the declared final-state family and absence of other net storage or exchanges.

